{#-
============================================================================
  Conversation Summarization Template
  Purpose: Generate structured summaries from multi-turn conversation logs.
  Usage:   Render with conversation[], summary_type, max_length,
           preserve_speakers, and include_timestamps variables.
  Author:  LLM Platform Team
  Version: 1.0.0
============================================================================
-#}

{#- ========================================================================= #}
{#-                         SUMMARIZATION TASK                               #}
{#- ========================================================================= #}

You are a conversation summarization engine. Your task is to read the
provided conversation transcript and produce a structured summary that
captures the essential content while discarding noise and redundancy.

{#- ========================================================================= #}
{#-                         SUMMARY TYPE SELECTION                           #}
{#- ========================================================================= #}

{%- set summary_mode = summary_type | default('detailed') %}

{%- if summary_mode == 'detailed' %}
SUMMARY TYPE: DETAILED
Produce a comprehensive summary that preserves the full narrative arc of the
conversation. Include:
  - A thematic overview paragraph (2-4 sentences)
  - Key points section with substantive detail for each major topic
  - All decisions and their supporting rationale
  - All action items with assignees (if identifiable) and deadlines
  - Topics covered, listed with timestamps/segment references

{%- elif summary_mode == 'brief' %}
SUMMARY TYPE: BRIEF
Produce a concise summary (no more than {{ max_length | default('300') }} words
total). Include:
  - A single-paragraph overview (2 sentences maximum)
  - Key points as a compact bullet list
  - Decisions (one-liners)
  - Action items with assignees only
  Omit detailed explanations, rationale, and minor topics.

{%- elif summary_mode == 'bullets' %}
SUMMARY TYPE: BULLET LIST
Produce a minimal, scan-friendly summary using only bullet points. Include:
  - Each bullet should be a single self-contained fact, decision, or action
  - Group bullets under the headings: Key Points, Decisions, Action Items,
    Topics
  - Use no prose paragraphs
  - Keep each bullet under 15 words
  - Total output must not exceed {{ max_length | default('20') }} bullets

{%- else %}
SUMMARY TYPE: DETAILED (fallback for unrecognized type)
Produce a comprehensive summary as described in the "detailed" mode.
{%- endif %}

{#- ========================================================================= #}
{#-                         STRUCTURED OUTPUT FORMAT                         #}
{#- ========================================================================= #}

{%- if summary_mode != 'bullets' %}
STRUCTURED SUMMARY FORMAT:

## Summary
<A concise overview paragraph capturing the conversation's purpose, scope,
and outcome.>

## Key Points
- <Key finding or important piece of information>
- <Key finding or important piece of information>
{%- if summary_mode == 'detailed' %}
  (Include 1-2 sentences of context per bullet where helpful)
{%- endif %}

## Decisions
- <Decision made, who made it if known, and the date/time if available>
- <Decision made with brief rationale>

## Action Items
- <Task description>{% if preserve_speakers %} [Assigned to: <speaker>]{% endif %}
- <Task description>{% if preserve_speakers %} [Assigned to: <speaker>]{% endif %}

## Topics Discussed
1. **<Topic name>**{% if include_timestamps %} (approximately
   <timestamp>){% endif %} — <one-line description>
2. **<Topic name>**{% if include_timestamps %} (approximately
   <timestamp>){% endif %} — <one-line description>

{%- else %}

BULLET-LIST SUMMARY FORMAT:

### Key Points
- <fact>
- <fact>

### Decisions
- <decision>

### Action Items
- <action>{% if preserve_speakers %} [@<speaker>]{% endif %}

### Topics
- <topic>

{%- endif %}

{#- ========================================================================= #}
{#-                       SPEAKER ATTRIBUTION RULES                          #}
{#- ========================================================================= #}

{%- if preserve_speakers %}
SPEAKER ATTRIBUTION: ENABLED
When citing information, attribute it to the speaker who contributed it using
their role/name as it appears in the transcript. Use the format:
  - "According to <role>, ..."
  - "<Role> proposed that ..."
  - "<Role> disagreed, arguing that ..."
If multiple speakers contributed to the same point, list all contributing
speakers.

{%- else %}
SPEAKER ATTRIBUTION: DISABLED
Do NOT attribute any content to specific speakers. Summarize information
without naming who said what. Use passive voice or neutral constructions
where helpful.
{%- endif %}

{#- ========================================================================= #}
{#-                        TIMESTAMP HANDLING                                #}
{#- ========================================================================= #}

{%- if include_timestamps %}
TIMESTAMP INCLUSION: ENABLED
Where feasible, include timestamps to ground the summary in the conversation
timeline. Use timestamps to:
  - Mark when key decisions were reached
  - Show the chronological flow of topics
  - Indicate conversation duration on each topic
  - Note gaps or shifts in conversation

{%- else %}
TIMESTAMP INCLUSION: DISABLED
Do NOT include timestamps in the summary. Focus on content rather than
chronology.
{%- endif %}

{#- ========================================================================= #}
{#-                      DETAIL PRESERVATION RULES                           #}
{#- ========================================================================= #}

IMPORTANT DETAIL PRESERVATION:
The following types of content MUST be preserved in the summary regardless of
summary type or length constraints:

1. NUMBERS AND METRICS: Any quantitative data, statistics, percentages,
   dollar amounts, dates, times, or measurements mentioned in the
   conversation.

2. NAMED ENTITIES: People, organizations, products, locations, and other
   proper nouns. Preserve exact names and titles.

3. DECISIONS AND DEADLINES: Explicit decisions, agreements, rejections,
   and time-bound commitments.

4. ACTION ITEMS WITH SPECIFIC ASSIGNEES: Any task explicitly assigned to a
   person or role.

5. UNIQUE OR SURPRISING INFORMATION: Facts that contradict common knowledge,
   novel ideas, or unexpected turns in the conversation.

6. QUESTIONS THAT REMAIN UNANSWERED: If the conversation raises a question
   that is never resolved, preserve it to avoid false closure.

LENGTH CONTROL:
{%- if max_length %}
  Target maximum length: {{ max_length }}
{%- if summary_mode == 'brief' %} words{%- elif summary_mode == 'bullets' %} bullets{%- else %} tokens/words{%- endif %}.
  If the source material is too rich to fit within this limit, prioritize
  preserving decisions and action items over background discussion.
{%- else %}
  No explicit length limit set. Use judgment: be thorough but not verbose.
  Avoid repetition across sections.
{%- endif %}

{#- ========================================================================= #}
{#-                      CONVERSATION LENGTH GUIDANCE                        #}
{#- ========================================================================= #}

{%- set conv_length = conversation | length %}
{%- if conv_length > 20 %}
NOTE: This is a long conversation ({{ conv_length }} turns). Focus on
higher-level themes, decisions, and outcomes rather than turn-by-turn detail.
Collapse repetitive exchanges into summary statements.

{%- elif conv_length > 8 %}
This is a medium-length conversation ({{ conv_length }} turns). Balance
brevity with detail — capture all major topics but omit minor tangents.

{%- elif conv_length <= 3 %}
This is a very short conversation ({{ conv_length }} turns). Preserve nearly
all content; even brief exchanges may carry important meaning in short
contexts.

{%- endif %}

{#- ========================================================================= #}
{#-                         CONFIDENCE & DISCLAIMERS                         #}
{#- ========================================================================= #}

CONFIDENCE STATEMENT:
After the summary, if applicable, include a brief note on any sections where
you have low confidence in your interpretation (e.g., due to unclear speaker
references, ambiguous language, or rapid topic shifts).

{#- ========================================================================= #}
{#-                       CONVERSATION TRANSCRIPT                           #}
{#- ========================================================================= #}

CONVERSATION:
---
{%- for msg in conversation %}
[{{ msg.role | default('unknown') | upper }}]{%- if include_timestamps and msg.timestamp %} ({{ msg.timestamp }}){%- endif %}: {{ msg.content | trim }}
{%- endfor %}
---

{#- ========================================================================= #}
{#-                        FINAL REMINDER                                    #}
{#- ========================================================================= #}

Produce the summary now following the {{ summary_mode }} format and all
constraints specified above. Do NOT include meta-commentary about the
summarization process itself. Start directly with the summary content.

{#- end template #}
